llama : without mmap, read the tensors in file order

#31321 (b12032) loads the tensors of a model read without mmap biggest-first,
so that the one staging buffer it now reuses only ever shrinks. On a network
file system read cold that order costs more than it saves: the reads jump
across the file instead of going through it once, and the client's
read-ahead stops helping. On LUMI-G (Lustre), the first round on b12032
loaded two Qwen3-235B Q4_K_M at once in 160 s, where the four rounds before
it on b11925 and earlier took 103-121 s, the same point each time;
the mmap variant of that point, 17 minutes before, ran past the warm-up's
time limit, and DeepSeek, Qwen3-32B split by tensor and the 235B with the
expert cache loaded slower too. On ROCm every one of these loads takes the
async upload, so the order is all b12032 changed for them.

The tensors now come sorted by file and offset, one pass from the first
byte to the last (before #31321 they came in the context's order, nearly
the same), and the staging buffer only grows, to the largest tensor: the
peak upstream's first allocation had anyway, without a reallocation, and its
page faults, at every size change.

EuLLM patch on llama.cpp b12032, after 0012 (which changes the same
function).

diff --git a/src/llama-model-loader.cpp b/src/llama-model-loader.cpp
index 6eeebb2c5..e773f8134 100644
--- a/src/llama-model-loader.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-model-loader.cpp
@@ -1788,16 +1788,20 @@ bool llama_model_loader::load_all_data(
         tensors.push_back(cur);
     }
 
-    // without mmap, tensors in non-host buffers are staged through a temporary buffer sized like the tensor
-    // load them biggest-first so the largest staging buffer is allocated while the fewest weights are resident
+    // EuLLM: without mmap, the tensors are read in the order their data lies in
+    // the files, one pass from the first byte to the last. Upstream reads them
+    // biggest-first (#31321), for a staging buffer that only shrinks; on a
+    // network file system read cold (Lustre on LUMI-G) the jumps that order
+    // makes cost more than the buffer saves: two Qwen3-235B loading together
+    // took 160 s on b12032 against 103-121 s before it. The staging buffer
+    // below grows to the largest tensor instead, the same peak as upstream's.
     if (!use_mmap) {
-        std::stable_sort(tensors.begin(), tensors.end(), [](const ggml_tensor * a, const ggml_tensor * b) {
-            const bool staged_a = a->buffer && !ggml_backend_buffer_is_host(a->buffer);
-            const bool staged_b = b->buffer && !ggml_backend_buffer_is_host(b->buffer);
-            if (staged_a != staged_b) {
-                return staged_a;
-            }
-            return staged_a && ggml_nbytes(a) > ggml_nbytes(b);
+        const auto file_position = [this](const ggml_tensor * t) {
+            const auto * w = get_weight(ggml_get_name(t));
+            return w ? std::make_pair((size_t) w->idx, w->offs) : std::make_pair(SIZE_MAX, (size_t) 0);
+        };
+        std::stable_sort(tensors.begin(), tensors.end(), [&](const ggml_tensor * a, const ggml_tensor * b) {
+            return file_position(a) < file_position(b);
         });
     }
 
@@ -1948,8 +1952,8 @@ bool llama_model_loader::load_all_data(
                     const size_t read_size      = (pad + n_size + align - 1) & ~(align - 1);
                     const size_t buf_size       = read_size + align - 1;
 
-                    // tensors come biggest-first: shrink the buffer when it gets too large, so peak memory stays close to one tensor
-                    if (read_buf.size() < buf_size || read_buf.size() > 2*buf_size) {
+                    // EuLLM: tensors come in file order, so the buffer only grows, to the largest tensor
+                    if (read_buf.size() < buf_size) {
                         // free the old buffer first, `read_buf = {}` would keep its capacity
                         read_buf = std::vector<no_init<uint8_t>>();
                         read_buf.resize(buf_size);
